<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jaén</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jaén is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and the capital of the province of Jaén, below the castle hill of Santa Catalina. Under Muslim rule it was a fortress town and for a time the seat of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a>, and in 1246 it was surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-of-castile">Ferdinand III of Castile</a>, becoming a key frontier stronghold against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>. Its cathedral is a landmark of Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> architecture.</p></div>
